# own_native.tkr — the HOST configuration of the own-backend corpus channel.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL (0.3.1 channel ruling, owner 2026-07-26). These scenarios used to live
# in regressor.tkr as `Given source` rows naming ONE case file, and the runner's snippet FOLD gave
# them one synthesized build per (source, configuration). That fold could never take a
# `Then object well-formed` row, so the riscv64 half of the corpus paid a SECOND build for the very
# same program. Making the corpus a PROJECT removes the fold from the picture entirely: a project
# `.tkr` is compiled at most once (`RegrBuilt`), every scenario of this file runs off that one build,
# and an object check inspects THAT build's own `.o`.
#
# ONE FILE PER CONFIGURATION, and that is not a stylistic choice: the project cache is keyed on the
# build SHAPE, not on the env, so the first scenario reaching it fixes the configuration for the whole
# file. A second target therefore needs a second `.tkr` (own_native_riscv64.tkr), never a second
# `Examples` row — a row that declared a different target would silently assert this file's HOST
# artifact.
#
# The three rows at the end name a `Given source` case file instead: each is a lone row of its own
# cross target, so a project build would buy nothing and a one-line program is the honest minimum for
# "does the emitter produce a well-formed object for that target".
Feature: F2 language corpus — folded EXPECT_EXIT single-file fixtures (dobrado, dir removido)

  Scenario: own_arith_exit (own-native — `n` is a `mut` local, so the MUL/ADD are fold-opaque)
    When built and run
    Then exit = 42

  Scenario: own_sub_exit (own-native — same fold-opacity rationale, MUL/SUB)
    When built and run
    Then exit = 42


